<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">Xochicalco</h1><p class="st-meta">650 CE – 900 CE (approx.) · capital Xochicalco</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A fortified hilltop city in Morelos that flourished after Teotihuacan's fall, famed for its Temple of the Feathered Serpent.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Xochicalco was built around 650 on a group of hills in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morelos">Morelos</a>, as power in central <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/mexico">Mexico</a> fragmented after the decline of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/teotihuacan">Teotihuacan</a>. Its position allowed control of trade routes between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valley-of-mexico">Valley of Mexico</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrero">Guerrero</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-coast-of-mexico">Gulf Coast</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The city was terraced, walled and moated, with three ballcourts, palaces and an observatory cave where a shaft allowed sunlight to mark the zenith passages of the sun. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temple-of-the-feathered-serpent-xochicalco">Temple of the Feathered Serpent</a> carries reliefs of plumed serpents and seated figures in Maya-like style, with glyphs that some scholars read as a calendar correction meeting. Artisans worked obsidian and other goods for trade.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Xochicalco was burnt and abandoned around 900. Its ideas, such as feathered-serpent imagery, passed to the later <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/toltec-state">Toltec state</a>. It became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 1999.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/xochicalco.jpg" alt="Xochicalco" width="320" height="240"><figcaption>Image: Maxtreiber · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mexico_xochicalco_pyramids.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>650 CE – 900 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Xochicalco</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Mesoamerican religion</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/mexico">Mexico</a></dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=775&amp;focus=xochicalco" aria-label="Xochicalco on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="1992 1481 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Xochicalco lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2312 1737h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2312 1737h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=775&amp;focus=xochicalco">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

